WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:10.372
The Pope-Leighey House is a striking example
of Frank Lloyd Wright’s work.

00:00:10.372 --> 00:00:14.730
A cypress and brick masterpiece so connected
to nature

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.334
it includes a kitchen window made for reaching
out and picking herbs.

00:00:19.334 --> 00:00:23.231
One of the highlights of this home is its
unusual windows.

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:25.513
These features are a striking sign 

00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:28.835
of the way Frank Lloyd Wright rethought the relationship

00:00:28.835 --> 00:00:31.421
between homes and natural light.

00:00:34.729 --> 00:00:37.887
If you walk through the galley from the entry
to the bedroom

00:00:37.887 --> 00:00:41.224
you’ll find light hung like pictures on
the wall.

00:00:42.276 --> 00:00:46.250
The most distinctive windows in this house,
the perforated windows

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:51.819
are called clerestory windows at the top,
an old technique Wright embraced.

00:00:51.819 --> 00:00:55.180
They bring natural light into the room from
ceiling height

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:59.180
preserving privacy and lighting the room more
broadly.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.730
The subtle effect echoes that of the recessed
lighting he favored.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:13.080
These perforations were designs Wright made
as an evolution of his stained glass windows

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:20.960
like these ones in a 1921 house Wright built.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.030
Each type of house like the Pope-Leighey house
received their own unique design.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:31.395
Later, he simplified even more with glass
set into concrete blocks.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.910
I could not believe that I found this model.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.010
Like, I was just there earlier this week

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:43.920
and then when I programmed in the exact 
date and time

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.840
that I shot the specific clip, at the house

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.084
your model matched the light perfectly.

00:01:54.351 --> 00:01:58.710
John Luttropp's painstakingly designed 3D
model of the Pope Leighey House

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.793
lets anyone play around with light.

00:02:00.793 --> 00:02:04.851
Then you can render a more realistic version
that is super accurate.

00:02:04.851 --> 00:02:13.209
If I shot the clip at 4:42, 4:29, the light
was off, but if I put in 4:42

00:02:13.209 --> 00:02:15.000
It was exactly correct.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.803
I mean, did you know that would happen?

00:02:17.511 --> 00:02:22.555
No, I had no idea that it was that accurate.

00:02:22.555 --> 00:02:30.489
What I usually do is, I try to find at least
the floor plan and the floor elevations

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:34.630
and then I’ll dig around for photos too
to get the details in.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.530
So, for the design of those perforated windows

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.950
were you able to just look at pictures and then 
in sketch up

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:45.834
kind of carve out the proper pattern?

00:02:45.834 --> 00:02:51.498
Yes, I found a very good drawing of it online
that was to scale

00:02:51.498 --> 00:02:54.591
and that I used as my basis for it.

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:59.599
These clerestory windows were combined with
other perforated windows of the same design

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:01.770
like these in the child’s bedroom.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:06.867
And large, wall-sized windows that let in
tons of light throughout the day.

00:03:06.867 --> 00:03:09.400
But all this would have been just a design
touch

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.000
if it weren’t at the center of a broader
attempt to make light part of the house.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.989
I didn’t think I was going to like the house
as much as I did.

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:27.813
I thought, well, it doesn't look like it's
a very big house, which it isn't.

00:03:27.813 --> 00:03:31.349
But it also didn't look like it had very good
layout

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:34.400
from what I could tell from just looking at
floor plans

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.057
and a few bad photographs that were out there
at the time.

00:03:38.057 --> 00:03:40.669
But being in the house... 

00:03:42.174 --> 00:03:45.076
was such a different experience.

00:03:45.076 --> 00:03:49.173
That entryway and then walk down 
into the living room.

00:03:49.173 --> 00:03:55.114
The tiny but really functional kitchen;
the office off to the side.

00:03:57.363 --> 00:03:58.659
And the fireplace.

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:03.424
Wright always builds a fireplace to be this
special thing

00:04:03.424 --> 00:04:09.660
this place where people can gather around
and in that house it really felt that way.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:15.500
This house is what Wright called a “Usonian”,
his model for a new American house.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.479
As an architect, Wright was famous for legendary
large projects from the Roble House...

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:24.629
to Tokyo’s imperial hotel...

00:04:24.629 --> 00:04:26.610
to the Johnson Wax headquarters.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.379
After earlier experiments 
in affordable housing.

00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:34.729
By the 1930s, he was seriously seeking to
bring his principles to more people.

00:04:35.128 --> 00:04:38.669
As he later wrote in “The Natural House”
he believed in

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:43.550
“a building as dignified as a tree in the
midst of nature.”

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.610
The Herbert Jacobs House in Madison, Wisconsin

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.860
is considered Wright’s first Usonian

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.819
and a Time magazine article about Wright and
that Jacobs House

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:58.573
inspired a DC area journalist to beg Wright
to build him a Usonian too.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:03.369
The Pope-Leighey house features many Wright
trademarks

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:05.020
that help the distinctive windows

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.020
light the house

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:08.740
“in God’s way — the natural way

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:13.826
as nearly as possible in the daytime
and at night as nearly like the day.”

00:05:13.826 --> 00:05:15.984
One is clearing obstructions.

00:05:15.984 --> 00:05:19.035
A relatively open floor plan lets light travel.

00:05:19.035 --> 00:05:22.059
See how the living room and dining area connect?

00:05:22.059 --> 00:05:27.530
Radiant floor heating means no radiators took
up space or obstructing light.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:30.099
Wright hated attics for a similar reason

00:05:30.099 --> 00:05:34.069
so he recommended people “get rid of the
attic” and, if they didn’t

00:05:34.069 --> 00:05:36.738
they use a “clerestory or lantern”

00:05:36.738 --> 00:05:41.669
Instead of a garage, this house features an
open air carport as well.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:45.900
Shade, via trellises, offered occupants flexibility.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:49.424
And perhaps most importantly, 
the unadorned walls

00:05:49.424 --> 00:05:54.270
no paint, no plaster, no curtains unless by
special request

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.688
put the pattern of light at the center of
the adornment for the house.

00:06:03.258 --> 00:06:06.279
This was a stark break from a lot of design
of the time

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:12.911
where relatively small windows were covered,
walls were plastered, and floors were carpeted.

00:06:12.911 --> 00:06:17.639
House Beautiful magazine put together demonstration
homes in the plaza hotel.

00:06:17.639 --> 00:06:20.228
They represented the prevailing aesthetic:

00:06:20.228 --> 00:06:25.126
closed floor plan, carpet, lots of curtains,
visible light fixtures

00:06:25.126 --> 00:06:27.542
basically anything that blocked light.

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:32.779
Though the Usonian challenged trends.

00:06:32.779 --> 00:06:34.389
It didn’t become ubiquitous.

00:06:34.389 --> 00:06:39.501
But the design meant light was as important
a material as brick and wood.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:48.106
Levittowns are some of the most famous suburban
mass developments in the United States.

00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:51.629
They were, in some ways, the antithesis of
Wright’s dream

00:06:51.629 --> 00:06:55.650
of organic interaction between a house and
nature.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.340
But developers William and Alfred Levitt’s
open floor plan design

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.827
was inspired by Wright’s approach.

00:07:01.827 --> 00:07:07.341
The “The Levittowner” in particular, copied
natural light design elements like carports

00:07:07.341 --> 00:07:10.308
window walls, and even high windows.

00:07:10.308 --> 00:07:15.399
But Wright's massive influence doesn’t capture
the scope of his philosophy.

00:07:15.399 --> 00:07:17.839
He hoped that architects would prioritize

00:07:17.839 --> 00:07:23.050
the “proper orientation of the house”
to make windows work well with the light.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:26.069
How did you figure out the orientation?

00:07:26.069 --> 00:07:29.339
The floor plan I had had the North arrow on
it.

00:07:29.339 --> 00:07:31.492
You know, that’s always helpful.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:38.289
The Pope Leighey house was actually moved
to its current location in Alexandria, Virginia.

00:07:38.851 --> 00:07:43.139
It was designed to face South, before being
built facing North West

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:47.129
and then reoriented slightly during moves
at Woodlawn.

00:07:47.129 --> 00:07:50.710
The light creates a different house not only
at different times of year.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.610
But in different locations.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:57.279
Frank Lloyd Wright didn’t just consider
the placement of the windows

00:07:57.279 --> 00:08:00.239
but also the position of the stars.

